Runtime thread-confinement checks in code: Static bytecode
analysis like ArchUnit cannot tell which thread executes a lambda at
runtime—it cannot distinguish IOThread calling
future.complete() on a background thread from queueing
it for tick(). The sixth rule therefore has to be
enforced in the code itself: TickCompletableFuture
captures the thread that created it and rejects
complete(), fail(), or
whenComplete() calls from any other thread:
class TickCompletableFuture
private final Thread ownerThread = Thread.currentThread();
private void checkOnOwnerThread() {
if (Thread.currentThread() != ownerThread) {
throw new IllegalStateException(
"TickCompletableFuture must be accessed on its owner (tick) thread ["
+ ownerThread.getName() + "], but was called from ["
+ Thread.currentThread().getName() + "]");
}
}
public void complete(T result) {
checkOnOwnerThread();
if (state != State.PENDING) {
throw new IllegalStateException("Future is already resolved");
}
this.state = State.COMPLETED;
this.result = result;
if (callback != null) {
callback.accept(result, null);
callback = null;
}
}
